</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7ec4fa170b4f467956fe17e57a429010bf1d822c" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -0.671ex; width:3.909ex; height:2.676ex;" alt="{\displaystyle \scriptstyle {\sqrt {(}}n)}"/></span> is impossible because the product of any two would be > <i id="mwLA">n</i>.</p>

<p id="mwLQ">When a trial division is successful, the process is applied <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recursion_(computer_science)" title="Recursion (computer science)" id="mwLg">recursively</a> to the resulting <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quotient" title="Quotient" id="mwLw">quotient</a>.</p>

<p id="mwMA">A definite bound on the prime factors is possible. Suppose <span class="texhtml mvar" style="font-style:italic;" about="#mwt23" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwMQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mvar","href":"./Template:Mvar"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"P&lt;sub>i&lt;/sub>"}},"i":0}}]}'>P<sub>i</sub></span> is the <span class="texhtml mvar" style="font-style:italic;" about="#mwt24" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwMg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mvar","href":"./Template:Mvar"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"i"}},"i":0}}]}'>i</span>'th prime, so that <i id="mwMw">P</i><sub id="mwNA">1</sub> = 2, <i id="mwNQ">P</i><sub id="mwNg">2</sub> = 3, <i id="mwNw">P</i><sub id="mwOA">3</sub> = 5, etc. Then the last prime number worth testing as a possible factor of <i id="mwOQ">n</i> is <span class="texhtml mvar" style="font-style:italic;" about="#mwt25" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwOg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mvar","href":"./Template:Mvar"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"P&lt;sub>i&lt;/sub>"}},"i":0}}]}'>P<sub>i</sub></span> where <span class="texhtml " about="#mwt26" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwOw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"math","href":"./Template:Math"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"&apos;&apos;P&apos;&apos;&lt;sup>2&lt;/sup>&lt;sub>&apos;&apos;i&apos;&apos;&amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp;1&lt;/sub> > &apos;&apos;n&apos;&apos;"}},"i":0}}]}'><i>P</i><sup>2</sup><sub><i>i</i><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>+<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1</sub> > <i>n</i></span>; equality here would mean that <span class="texhtml " about="#mwt27" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwPA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"math","href":"./Template:Math"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"&apos;&apos;P&apos;&apos;&lt;sub>&apos;&apos;i&apos;&apos;&amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp;1&lt;/sub>"}},"i":0}}]}'><i>P</i><sub><i>i</i><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>+<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1</sub></span> is a factor. Thus, testing with 2, 3, and 5 suffices up to <i id="mwPQ">n</i> = 48 not just 25 because the square of the next prime is 49, and below <i id="mwPg">n</i> = 25 just 2 and 3 are sufficient. Should the square root of <i id="mwPw">n</i> be an integer, then it is a factor and <i id="mwQA">n</i> is a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Square_number" title="Square number" id="mwQQ">perfect square</a>.</p>

<p id="mwQg">The trial division algorithm in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pseudocode" title="Pseudocode" id="mwQw">pseudocode</a>:</p>

<pre id="mwRA"><b id="mwRQ">algorithm</b> trial-division <b id="mwRg">is</b>
    <b id="mwRw">input:</b> Integer <i id="mwSA">n</i> to be factored
    <b id="mwSQ">output:</b> List <i id="mwSg">F</i> of prime factors of <i id="mwSw">n</i>

    <i id="mwTA">P</i> ← set of all primes <span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwTQ">≤</span> <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt28" id="mwTg" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"{{\\sqrt{n}}}"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle {\sqrt {n}}}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
          <msqrt>
            <mi>n</mi>
          </msqrt>
        </mrow>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle {\sqrt {n}}}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/2a2994734eae382ce30100fb17b9447fd8e99f81" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -1.005ex; width:3.331ex; height:3.009ex;" alt="{\displaystyle {\sqrt {n}}}"/></span>
    <i id="mwTw">F</i> ← empty list of factors

    <b id="mwUA">for each</b> prime <i id="mwUQ">p</i> <b id="mwUg">in</b> <i id="mwUw">P</i> <b id="mwVA">do</b>
        <b id="mwVQ">while</b> <i id="mwVg">n</i> <b id="mwVw">mod</b> <i id="mwWA">p</i> is 0
            Add factor <i id="mwWQ">p</i> to list <i id="mwWg">F</i>
            <i id="mwWw">n</i> ← <i id="mwXA">n</i>/<i id="mwXQ">p</i>

    <b id="mwXg">if</b> <i id="mwXw">F</i> is empty    <i id="mwYA">(Original n is prime?)</i>
        Add factor <i id="mwYQ">n</i> to list <i id="mwYg">F</i></pre>

<p id="mwYw">Determining the primes less than or equal to <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt29" id="mwZA" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"{{\\sqrt{n}}}"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle {\sqrt {n}}}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
          <msqrt>
            <mi>n</mi>
          </msqrt>
        </mrow>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle {\sqrt {n}}}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/2a2994734eae382ce30100fb17b9447fd8e99f81" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -1.005ex; width:3.331ex; height:3.009ex;" alt="{\displaystyle {\sqrt {n}}}"/></span> is not a trivial task as <i id="mwZQ">n</i> gets larger, so the simplest computer programs to factor a number just try successive integers, prime and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composite_number" title="Composite number" id="mwZg">composite</a>, from 2 to <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt30" id="mwZw" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"{{\\sqrt{n}}}"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle {\sqrt {n}}}">
  <semantics>
